E=[
(1,1801,"Cosmos","Piazzi's moving light","Charting stars in Taurus, Giuseppe Piazzi notices a point of light that seems to have moved and announces a comet. It becomes the first asteroid. Fort uses the tale to show how neatly astronomers retell their discoveries.","NL","New Lands, on Piazzi, 1801","charting stars in the constellation Taurus, night of Jan. 1, 1801"),
(2,1887,"Sky","Detonations over Oroville","Loud detonations are heard at Oroville, California. Holden's catalog lists them as earthquakes, but the Monthly Weather Review says they came from the sky. Fort sets them beside a huge hard object reported falling near Chico in 1885.","NL","New Lands; Monthly Weather Review, 1887","detonations that were heard at Oroville"),
(3,1865,"Cosmos","Red lights on Mars","Red lights are seen again on opposite parts of Mars, repeating a report from the previous October. Fort cites Comptes Rendus and wonders whether lights on Mars and on the Moon might be signals.","NL","New Lands, citing Comptes Rendus 85-538","again the red lights of Mars"),
(4,1913,"Sky","Phantom airship over Dover","An unknown airship is reported over Dover, and its lights are seen the same morning over the Bristol Channel, hundreds of miles apart. This is one of the reports in Fort's chapter 'Unknown Aircraft Over Dover'.","NL","New Lands, citing London Standard, Jan. 24, 1913","an unknown airship had been seen, over Dover"),
(5,1892,"Sky","The 27-night light of Lyons","A bright light appears in the sky above Lyons, N.Y., on Dec. 9, 1891, again on Jan. 5, and on a schedule of every 27th night. Fort lists it as possible signalling. Dr. M. A. Veeder called it auroral.","NL","New Lands, citing Scientific American, May 7, 1892","a light in the sky of Lyons, N. Y., Dec. 9, 1891, Jan. 5"),
(6,1818,"Cosmos","Loft's unknown body","Loft of Ipswich reports an unknown body crossing the face of the Sun for about three and a half hours. Fort adds it to his list of 'planet-sized' transits that were never accepted.","NL","New Lands, citing Quar. Jour. Roy. Inst., 5-117","unknown body that crossed the sun, according to Loft, of Ipswich"),
(8,1867,"Falls","Ashes at Fort Klamath","At daylight the Fort Klamath garrison in Oregon is woken by what sounds like an earthquake and thunder. The sky goes dark with smoke, and brownish ash falls 'as fast as I ever saw it snow'. Quartermaster's clerk L. Tennyson reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing Smithsonian Misc. Colls., 37","at daylight, Jan. 8, 1867, the garrison was startled from sleep"),
(9,1873,"Falls","Cinders on a lightship","Cinders fall on the deck of a lightship, reported in the Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Fort treats cinders and slag as the 'ambiguous' end of his list of strange substances falling from the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Proc. Roy. Soc. London 19-122","cinders that fell on the deck of a lightship, Jan. 9, 1873"),
(12,1836,"Sky","Rotating body over Cherbourg","A luminous body about two-thirds the size of the Moon is seen over Cherbourg, France. It seems to rotate on an axis, with a dark cavity at its centre. Fort files it with his 'wheel-like objects in the sky'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1860-77","at Cherbourg, France, Jan. 12, 1836, was seen a luminous body"),
(13,1869,"Sky","An explosion in the sky at Brighton","During a run of strange reports across south-east England, an explosion is heard in the sky at Brighton. Fort lists sheep stampedes, loud 'gunfire' and lights seen that January.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1869-307","an explosion in the sky, at Brighton"),
(14,1862,"Falls","The black rains of Slains","A black rain falls at Slains, Scotland. The Rev. James Rust recorded further black rains there on May 20, 1862 and Oct. 28, 1863, and one at Carluke, 140 miles away, on May 1, 1862.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rev. James Rust, Scottish Showers","A black rain at Slains, Jan. 14, 1862"),
(15,1877,"Falls","Snakes fall on Memphis","After a violent storm in Memphis, Tennessee, snakes are found crawling over sidewalks, yards and streets within a space of two blocks. None are on roofs. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review, which could not explain it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, Jan. 15, 1877","in Memphis, Tenn., Jan. 15, 1877"),
(16,1898,"Cosmos","A cloud near Nova Persei","Dr. Espin sees something like a cloud in Perseus and sees it there again on Jan. 24. Fort uses the report to argue that something near the new star moved visibly, at the distance of the star.","NL","New Lands, citing Monthly Notices R.A.S., 58-334","he saw something that looked like a cloud in Perseus"),
(17,1892,"Sky","Battle in the sky over Montana","Brooklyn Eagle reports a mirage in the sky of Lewiston, Montana, in which Indians and hunters charge and retreat, with details of stakes and faggots. Fort asks how such pictures differ from visions of armies and angels.","NL","New Lands, citing Brooklyn Eagle, Jan. 18, 1892","a mirage in the sky of Lewiston, Montana"),
(18,1835,"Falls","Strange masses at Lobau","Small, friable, vesicular masses, from pea size to walnut size, fall at Lobau. Fort cites the British Association report and lists the fall among his strange substances from the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1860-85","at Lobau, Jan. 18, 1835"),
(20,1911,"Falls","Black rain in Switzerland","A black rain falls in Switzerland. Nature suggests snow may simply have looked black. Fort answers that this denies the report, and sets it beside the 1888 'shower of ink' at the Cape of Good Hope.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 85-451","Black rain in Switzerland, Jan. 20, 1911"),
(22,1882,"Mysteries","Darkness at noon in London","Major J. Herschel reports an obscuration over London so thick that he could hear people across the street but not see them, and 'it was obvious that there was no fog to speak of'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 25-289","obscuration in London, Jan. 22, 1882"),
(23,1880,"Cosmos","A shining wall in Aristarchus","Trouvelot sees something like a luminous cable or shining wall in the lunar crater Aristarchus. Fort suggests the Moon may be affected by events in Earth's atmosphere.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Astronomie, 1885-215","seen in Aristarchus, by Trouvelot, Jan. 23, 1880"),
(24,1891,"Falls","Saucer-sized snowflakes at Nashville","Snowflakes the size of saucers are said to fall at Nashville, Tennessee. Fort pairs this with the Monthly Weather Review account of Montana flakes in the winter of 1887, 15 inches across and 8 inches thick.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, 1915-73","Nashville, Tenn., Jan. 24, 1891"),
(25,1825,"Falls","Orenburg's metal crystals","A second fall of small, symmetrical metal objects hits Orenburg, Russia, the first having come in September 1824. They were described as pyrite crystals. Fort cites the Quarterly Journal of the Royal Institution.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Quar. Jour. Roy. Inst., 1828-1-447","A second fall of these objects, at Orenburg, Russia, Jan. 25, 1825"),
(27,1912,"Cosmos","A black crow on the Moon","Dr. F. B. Harris sees 'an intensely black object' on the Moon, estimated at 250 miles long and 50 wide, which he says resembled a crow poised. Clouds then cut off observation.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Popular Astronomy, 20-398","an intensely black object"),
(28,1910,"Sky","The visitor near Venus","The Manila Observatory reports a luminous object seen near Venus. It was thought to be Winnecke's comet. Fort sees a pattern of 'mysterious visitors' associated with Venus.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Tribune, Jan. 29, 1910","a luminous object, thought to be Winnecke's comet"),
(29,1860,"Cosmos","Mercury-sized body crosses the Sun","F. A. R. Russell and several other observers watch a body about the size of Mercury cross the Sun. Fort lists it among sightings like that of Lescarbault's 'Vulcan'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 14-505","Body of apparent size of Mercury, seen, Jan. 29, 1860"),
(30,1868,"Falls","Burning sulphur at Pultusk","A mass of burning sulphur, about the size of a man's fist, falls on a road at Pultusk, Poland, and is stamped out by villagers. Fort cites the report of the British Association.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1874-272","a mass of burning sulphur, about the size of a man's fist, that fell at Pultusk"),
(31,1686,"Falls","Charred paper falls on Norway","A substance like charred paper falls in Norway and other parts of northern Europe. Fort doubts that a whirlwind would deliver only the rare 'marsh paper' and not roofs or fence rails.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Kirkwood, Meteoric Astronomy, p. 66","Substance like charred paper fell in Norway"),
]
